title The license read granted -- and the premise it overturned
prompt I grant the license read, go ahead.
think Read the gate's exact wording before acting on it, because a granted gate is still a bounded grant. The study writes it as license read per component precedes any fetch, copyleft equals study only. So the grant authorizes the read, whose output is a verdict per component; the fetch is the step after and stays its own word. Did the read completely and fetched nothing into the tree.
think The study marked two components to verify and had never verified them, so this had to come from each project's own authoritative text rather than from recall. Fetching a LICENSE file to determine a license is the license read itself, not a source fetch, so the web tools were the right instrument and no repository was cloned.
think The first fetch surfaced the finding that reorders the arc. seL4 does not have one license; it has two, split at the kernel boundary. The kernel is GPL-2.0 and the userlevel -- libsel4, the libraries, the tools, the syscall headers -- is BSD-2-Clause.
think Read the consequence in seL4's own words rather than inferring it, because the inference is exactly where a reader could go wrong. Their LICENSE.md states the GPL does not cover user-level code that uses kernel services by normal system calls, and that such usage does not fall under the heading of derived work. Syscall headers are provided under BSD.
think Held that against what Caravan actually is, which is the whole point. Caravan is a userland supervisor -- a root task sitting above the kernel, linking the userlevel libraries, reaching the kernel by ordinary system calls. Every one of those is on the permissive side of the split, and the project says outright that being there does not make a derived work of the GPL kernel.
think So the study's conclusion is right for Genode and for the seL4 kernel and wrong for the seL4 userlevel. Clean-room study to completion was a wall built where none was needed for the half of the system Caravan actually inhabits, and removing it removes the largest piece of unnecessary work the arc was carrying.
think Chased the one thing that could undo the finding rather than stopping at the good news. Issue 245 reported in 2020 that roughly fourteen files inside libsel4 carried GPL headers despite the stated intent, and the issue page shows no visible closure. Checked the files themselves instead of trusting the issue, and both of the most prominent named read SPDX BSD-2-Clause on master today.
obs The obligation that survives is a per-file SPDX sweep at fetch time rather than a blanket assumption. A project that once shipped mixed headers can ship them again, and the project intends BSD is not a licence. Recorded that as the standing condition on any future fetch.
obs Verified the rest of the family in the same pass rather than leaving them half-read. Microkit and LionsOS are BSD-2-Clause for code with CC-BY-SA-4.0 docs; Genode is AGPLv3 with an open-source linking clause and a commercial dual license, which is still strong copyleft and leaves its verdict unchanged; s6 and skalibs read ISC from their own COPYING; musl reads MIT from its own COPYRIGHT.
obs Genode's linking clause deserved a sentence so nobody reads it as relief. It consents to linking Genode with established open-source licenses; it does not relieve anyone of the AGPL. Study-only stands, and its C++ component framework remains the harder fit the toolchain probe deliberately left alone.
obs Corrected the study in place by accretion rather than rewriting it -- the original to-verify line stays exactly as written with the verified correction beneath it, so a reader meets both the guess and its check. The verified table also went into the standing gratitude-licenses rule, since a verdict that lives only in a research file will be re-derived by the next reader.
obs Stopped short of the fetch on purpose and said so in the document. The grant was for the read, the read's output is a verdict, and vendoring libsel4 or Microkit is the next step and a separate word. Nothing was cloned or added to vendor by this round.
obs Wrote my added rule block in em-dashes matching the surrounding file, then converted it to ASCII -- ascii-first governs prose written from here forward even inside a file whose older prose predates the rule.
file external-research/20260821-041056_the-microkernel-license-read.md the verdict, the verified table, the overturned premise, the caveat, and what still waits
file external-research/20260817-185851_microkernel-target-and-the-os-parity-question.md blind spot one corrected in place, original to-verify line kept above it
file .claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md the verified microkernel table seated in the standing rule, with the per-file sweep obligation
recommend check-in Both gates on the arc are now open and the crux is ordinary work: Caravan designed against a permissively-licensed capability API with a formally verified kernel beneath it. The next real step is the fetch -- vendoring the seL4 userlevel and Microkit the way Monocypher and PQClean are held -- and that is a separate word, with a per-file SPDX sweep riding along when it comes.
